I'm hungry.

250
00:09:58,270 --> 00:10:00,164
There better be some pie left.

251
00:10:00,274 --> 00:10:01,920
Oh. Is that Tyler's brother?

252
00:10:02,061 --> 00:10:03,379
No, that's Tyler.

253
00:10:04,804 --> 00:10:05,923
Um...

254
00:10:06,046 --> 00:10:08,731
How old is he, exactly?

255
00:10:08,871 --> 00:10:11,970
14, but we don't trust him
to stay home alone.

256
00:10:13,180 --> 00:10:14,513
Oh, hold on. I'm not sure--

257
00:10:14,602 --> 00:10:16,883
Now there's a few basic things
you should know.

258
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:18,271
He can't drink pop,

259
00:10:18,562 --> 00:10:21,338
or he will freak out.

260
00:10:21,422 --> 00:10:23,628
And absolutely
no ice of any kind,

261
00:10:23,732 --> 00:10:25,647
or he will freak out.

262
00:10:25,741 --> 00:10:27,891
And don't let him watch
anything too violent...

263
00:10:27,982 --> 00:10:29,019
Or even too colorful...

264
00:10:29,116 --> 00:10:30,551
Or he will freak out.

265
00:10:30,657 --> 00:10:32,970
And he has to put on
his pull-ups before bed,

266
00:10:33,073 --> 00:10:35,373
but you can't call 'em diapers,

267
00:10:35,474 --> 00:10:36,901
or he will freak out.

268
00:10:37,020 --> 00:10:38,878
They're his "space pants."

269
00:10:39,003 --> 00:10:40,215
Okay.

270
00:10:40,340 --> 00:10:42,402
Well, then, that's it.
We'll be home around 10:00.

271
00:10:42,521 --> 00:10:44,250
Oh, uh, one more thing.

272
00:10:44,370 --> 00:10:47,128
Tyler thinks
he's babysitting you,

273
00:10:47,222 --> 00:10:50,239
and if he finds out that you're
babysitting him, he will--

274
00:10:50,328 --> 00:10:51,395
Freak out?

275
00:10:51,621 --> 00:10:53,581
<i>Big</i> freak-out.

276
00:10:53,720 --> 00:10:55,372
<i>Huge.</i>

277
00:10:55,961 --> 00:10:57,625
- Ready?
- Okay.

278
00:11:09,015 --> 00:11:11,220
Uh, Tyler?
Whatcha watching?

279
00:11:11,352 --> 00:11:12,773
"Zombie Makeout Party."

280
00:11:12,867 --> 00:11:14,725
First they kill you,
then they kiss you.

281
00:11:14,812 --> 00:11:17,204
Whoa. You know you are not
allowed to watch that stuff.

282
00:11:17,363 --> 00:11:20,775
Hey, you're not in charge.
I am.

283
00:11:21,939 --> 00:11:24,546
That's right, Tyler.
You're in charge.

284
00:11:24,829 --> 00:11:26,097
And as my babysitter,

285
00:11:26,195 --> 00:11:28,385
you shouldn't let me
watch that stuff.

286
00:11:29,694 --> 00:11:31,765
I don't deal well
with zombie movies.

287
00:11:31,980 --> 00:11:33,290
Then go to bed.

288
00:11:33,413 --> 00:11:35,415
Well, that's the thing.

289
00:11:35,550 --> 00:11:39,878
I don't have a bed, so why
don't I sleep here on the couch

290
00:11:39,969 --> 00:11:42,944
while you go up to your bed
and babysit me from up there?

291
00:11:43,046 --> 00:11:44,834
Will you stop whining?

292
00:11:51,341 --> 00:11:52,843
What's that?

293
00:11:53,119 --> 00:11:56,605
Oh. My ortho says I have to wear
this at least ten hours a night.

294
00:11:56,754 --> 00:12:01,404
Is there anything special
you need to wear at night?

295
00:12:02,019 --> 00:12:03,300
No.

296
00:12:04,776 --> 00:12:05,964
Really?

297
00:12:06,073 --> 00:12:07,453
'Cause...

298
00:12:08,742 --> 00:12:11,660
Okay, Tyler. Well...

299
00:12:12,357 --> 00:12:14,635
I'm gonna let you in
on a little secret.

300
00:12:14,925 --> 00:12:17,404
I'm a bed wetter.

301
00:12:17,512 --> 00:12:19,989
Yep, so every night,
before I go to sleep,

302
00:12:20,084 --> 00:12:23,159
I put on my high-tech
"space pants,"

303
00:12:23,284 --> 00:12:29,390
which keep me nice and dry
as I blast off into my dreams.

304
00:12:31,040 --> 00:12:32,082
You're a nerd up there

305
00:12:32,195 --> 00:12:34,182
<i>and</i> a nerd down there.

306
00:12:38,991 --> 00:12:41,155
Boom! I win!
What's the next game, huh? Ooh.

307
00:12:44,532 --> 00:12:46,037
Boom! I win!

308
00:12:47,074 --> 00:12:48,915
Boom! I win! Ooh!

309
00:12:49,840 --> 00:12:51,749
Boom! I win. Ha ha!

310
00:12:53,421 --> 00:12:54,489
Your turn.

311
00:12:54,577 --> 00:12:56,127
I get it. Boom. You win.

312
00:12:56,213 --> 00:12:57,484
What? You can't quit now.

313
00:12:57,569 --> 00:12:59,328
I've only crushed you
in nine out of ten games.

314
00:12:59,529 --> 00:13:02,167
I did better than I thought
I'd do. Good night.

315
00:13:02,322 --> 00:13:03,836
Fine.

316
00:13:07,676 --> 00:13:09,528
Thanks. Can I get a boost?

317
00:13:09,617 --> 00:13:12,273
What? No.
We're just pausing the game.

318
00:13:12,393 --> 00:13:14,114
Then where am <i>I</i>
supposed to sleep?

319
00:13:30,421 --> 00:13:31,947
What's all this?

320
00:13:32,038 --> 00:13:34,670
I found my business, Mike.
My business is beading.

321
00:13:34,751 --> 00:13:37,312
I mean, how many times have I
said I wanted to bead things?

322
00:13:37,427 --> 00:13:39,938
I don't know.
Zero? A thousand?

323
00:13:40,026 --> 00:13:41,878
But I never did
a thing about it.

324
00:13:41,971 --> 00:13:43,982
I just don't want to get to
the end of my life and look back

325
00:13:44,066 --> 00:13:46,524
and think, "Wow,
I had a shot at doing

326
00:13:46,595 --> 00:13:49,041
the one thing I really love,
and I didn't take it."

327
00:13:49,651 --> 00:13:50,441
Beading?

328
00:13:50,527 --> 00:13:51,791
I could even take these
to craft shows.

329
00:13:51,887 --> 00:13:54,778
This could be huge. I just
gotta get on Oprah's radar.

330
00:13:56,138 --> 00:13:57,952
You're gonna
give this to Oprah?

331
00:13:58,141 --> 00:14:00,494
Well, obviously,
I'd make one special for her.

332
00:14:00,588 --> 00:14:02,787
Hey, maybe you could sell these
to the guys at the quarry

333
00:14:02,875 --> 00:14:04,440
for their wives.

334
00:14:04,954 --> 00:14:06,072
Good night.

335
00:14:06,153 --> 00:14:08,388
Getting my business done.

336
00:14:08,573 --> 00:14:10,155
♪ Getting my business done ♪

337
00:14:12,157 --> 00:14:13,317
Crap.

338
00:14:15,182 --> 00:14:16,002
Unh!

339
00:14:16,733 --> 00:14:17,842
Ow.

340
00:14:18,578 --> 00:14:20,220
Good. You're awake.

341
00:14:20,378 --> 00:14:21,600
Time to play air hockey.

342
00:14:21,823 --> 00:14:24,407
Fine, but then can I
go back to my box?

343
00:14:31,702 --> 00:14:32,966
I wasn't ready.

344
00:14:37,107 --> 00:14:38,686
Okay. Beginner's luck.

345
00:14:41,297 --> 00:14:42,755
All right, dude,
what are you doing?!

346
00:14:42,892 --> 00:14:44,227
Oh, it's easy.
It's all angles.

347
00:14:44,334 --> 00:14:46,926
What? You can't use math.
That's cheating. Wh--

348
00:14:48,599 --> 00:14:51,416
Ohh, that's it!
Here comes the pain!

349
00:14:52,808 --> 00:14:53,598
Unh!

350
00:14:55,958 --> 00:14:57,545
Boom. I win.

351
00:15:01,465 --> 00:15:03,716
<i>After four days,</i>
<i>Sue finally started to recover</i>

352
00:15:03,811 --> 00:15:06,705
<i>from what came to be known</i>
<i>as the Tyler incident.</i>

353
00:15:06,961 --> 00:15:08,164
Morning!

354
00:15:08,282 --> 00:15:10,208
Sue, a Mrs. Jennings called.

355
00:15:10,305 --> 00:15:11,614
She wants to know
if "You're available for Tyler

356
00:15:11,701 --> 00:15:14,976
to babysit you on Saturday."
Must have wrote that down wrong.

357
00:15:15,081 --> 00:15:18,010
Ugh! No! I don't want
to go back there.

358
00:15:18,981 --> 00:15:21,300
But I guess I should.

359
00:15:21,390 --> 00:15:24,521
This is my business, and I am
going to need the referrals.

360
00:15:27,837 --> 00:15:29,183
I figured it out, Mike,

361
00:15:29,338 --> 00:15:32,096
and here's the thing--
I was being too selfish.

362
00:15:32,178 --> 00:15:34,788
The only one who benefits
from my beading is me.

363
00:15:35,046 --> 00:15:35,947
Agreed.

364
00:15:36,040 --> 00:15:37,236
So then I got to thinking,

365
00:15:37,327 --> 00:15:41,445
maybe my business should be
other people's business.

366
00:15:41,642 --> 00:15:42,940
Uh-huh.

367
00:15:43,035 --> 00:15:43,795
Look at this.

368
00:15:43,900 --> 00:15:46,310
I Googled the word "sad,"
and a million things came up.

369
00:15:46,406 --> 00:15:48,772
Orphans, stray animals,
old people--

370
00:15:48,874 --> 00:15:51,014
The whole world needs my help.

371
00:15:51,503 --> 00:15:52,845
Your shirt's inside out.

372
00:15:53,207 --> 00:15:54,560
Here's what I'm thinking.

373
00:15:54,666 --> 00:15:56,802
Kids love animals.
Old people love kids.

374
00:15:56,880 --> 00:15:59,476
Why are we still building
separate orphanages, pounds,

375
00:15:59,579 --> 00:16:03,193
and retirement homes? Seriously,
let's get them together.

376
00:16:03,287 --> 00:16:05,494
Why hasn't anybody
thought of this before?

377
00:16:05,600 --> 00:16:06,625
Huh?
That's what I'm gonna do.

378
00:16:06,732 --> 00:16:10,078
I am gonna build
the world's first super-shelter.

379
00:16:10,202 --> 00:16:11,913
First...
I gotta pick a location--

380
00:16:12,008 --> 00:16:14,711
Something that's convenient
for both humans <i>and</i> animals.

381
00:16:14,808 --> 00:16:16,166
Then I gotta get bids
from contractors

382
00:16:16,261 --> 00:16:17,258
and sign up volunteers.

383
00:16:17,353 --> 00:16:19,528
Oh, well, first I need
to write a grant proposal.

384
00:16:19,637 --> 00:16:20,948
I gotta call congress.

385
00:16:23,573 --> 00:16:24,576
Game.

386
00:16:24,666 --> 00:16:25,521
Rematch!

387
00:16:25,604 --> 00:16:27,240
I've already given you
a hundred rematches.

388
00:16:27,334 --> 00:16:28,537
I'm retiring my paddle.

389
00:16:29,025 --> 00:16:30,585
Okay, just hear me out.

390
00:16:30,695 --> 00:16:33,666
If you beat me, I'll do
your homework for a month.

391
00:16:34,552 --> 00:16:36,890
Thanks, but I'd like
to see the fifth grade.

392
00:16:37,027 --> 00:16:39,359
Okay, I won't make you
do <i>my</i> homework for a month.

393
00:16:42,658 --> 00:16:44,960
Throw in this guitar pick,
and you're on.

394
00:16:45,482 --> 00:16:47,304
Fine, but what do you
want my pick for?

395
00:16:47,941 --> 00:16:49,236
Oh, I like to lick it.

396
00:16:49,342 --> 00:16:51,521
This way, I don't have to
do it in secret anymore.

397
00:16:53,803 --> 00:16:55,929
<i>Come on.</i>
<i>What's my business, damn it?</i>

398
00:16:56,051 --> 00:16:58,106
<i>What, what, what, what, what?</i>

399
00:16:58,572 --> 00:17:00,573
<i>Everyone else is shoving</i>
<i>their business in my face.</i>

400
00:17:00,676 --> 00:17:01,649
<i>Rude.</i>

401
00:17:01,800 --> 00:17:03,316
<i>If I could just fall asleep,</i>

402
00:17:03,418 --> 00:17:05,087
<i>maybe it'd come to me</i>
<i>in a dream.</i>

403
00:17:05,938 --> 00:17:07,644
<i>What's that pounding?</i>

404
00:17:07,814 --> 00:17:10,214
<i>I could make first aid kits</i>
<i>for soldiers overseas.</i>

405
00:17:10,314 --> 00:17:11,762
<i>I left my purse in the car.</i>

406
00:17:11,866 --> 00:17:13,927
<i>Should I go get it?</i>
<i>I should go get it.</i>

407
00:17:14,037 --> 00:17:17,021
<i>But that woman down the street</i>
<i>got mugged. Hope she's okay.</i>

408
00:17:17,160 --> 00:17:19,606
<i>Did I floss? I need a beer.</i>

409
00:17:19,715 --> 00:17:21,100
<i>Seriously,</i>
<i>what is that pounding?</i>

410
00:17:21,215 --> 00:17:23,269
<i>Is that my pulse?</i>
<i>It's like a hammer.</i>

411
00:17:23,380 --> 00:17:25,944
<i>Am I supposed to feel</i>
<i>my heartbeat in my tongue?</i>

412
00:17:26,055 --> 00:17:28,834
<i>That's gotta be wrong.</i>
<i>What's happening to me? Okay.</i>

413
00:17:28,927 --> 00:17:31,831
<i>Calm down.</i>
<i>Calm down. Calm down.</i>

414
00:17:33,808 --> 00:17:36,524
I'm going to get band-aids
for my soldier first aid kits!

415
00:17:38,049 --> 00:17:40,664
<i>Run! It's a makeout zombie!</i>

416
00:17:40,827 --> 00:17:43,690
<i>The makeout zombie!</i>

417
00:17:53,637 --> 00:17:54,875
Sticky gauzes...

418
00:17:55,021 --> 00:17:56,727
Unsticky gauzes...

419
00:17:58,031 --> 00:18:00,922
Oh, God. What am I doing?
The army <i>has</i> first aid kits.

420
00:18:01,026 --> 00:18:03,124
Okay, what else?
What else can I do?

421
00:18:03,253 --> 00:18:05,660
First aid kits
for animals at the pound.

422
00:18:05,912 --> 00:18:07,573
Excuse me.
Where's your pet section?

423
00:18:07,658 --> 00:18:09,578
I need dog thermometers.
Do they even make those?

424
00:18:09,661 --> 00:18:10,961
Or should I just use
baby thermometers?

425
00:18:11,056 --> 00:18:12,237
And what about
something for cats?

426
00:18:12,341 --> 00:18:13,641
Do you have something
for homeless cats

427
00:18:13,720 --> 00:18:15,050
so that they can do this?

428
00:18:15,163 --> 00:18:16,279
Um, are you feeling okay,
ma'am?

429
00:18:16,427 --> 00:18:17,228
Kind of no.

430
00:18:17,340 --> 00:18:19,053
I can hear my heartbeat
like a thump, thump, thump.

431
00:18:19,148 --> 00:18:20,516
Just-- just take my pulse.

432
00:18:20,613 --> 00:18:23,590
Um... I'm actually not allowed
to touch the customers anymore.

433
00:18:24,417 --> 00:18:25,846
But we got
a blood pressure chair.

434
00:18:27,643 --> 00:18:29,992
Come on.
Just one more game?

435
00:18:30,093 --> 00:18:31,552
I flipped through
my geometry book in the john

436
00:18:31,662 --> 00:18:33,822
and totally figured out angles.

437
00:18:33,952 --> 00:18:35,654
But you have
nothing left I want.

438
00:18:35,841 --> 00:18:37,763
Really? Nothing?

439
00:18:40,515 --> 00:18:42,820
Well, there is <i>one</i> thing.

440
00:18:44,417 --> 00:18:45,265
Dad!

441
00:18:45,365 --> 00:18:48,476
I'm babysitting a 14-year-old,
and he thinks we're on a date.

442
00:18:52,615 --> 00:18:54,553
All right. I'm taking it
from here. Go on home.

443
00:18:54,653 --> 00:18:55,991
Okay, but there's a bunch
of things you need to know.

444
00:18:56,083 --> 00:18:57,811
He can't have ice,
and he has to wear his pull-ups,

445
00:18:57,890 --> 00:18:58,549
but you can't call 'em--

446
00:18:58,626 --> 00:19:00,589
Sue, I raised three kids.
I'll be fine.

447
00:19:03,291 --> 00:19:05,369
Okay, Tyler! Time for bed!

448
00:19:05,458 --> 00:19:07,515
Turn off the TV
and put on your diapers!

449
00:19:11,877 --> 00:19:12,827
Yikes.

450
00:19:12,927 --> 00:19:14,621
160 over 102?

451
00:19:15,187 --> 00:19:16,696
Time to cut down
on the cookie dough.

452
00:19:20,007 --> 00:19:22,730
Okay. Message received.
You can let me go now.

453
00:19:26,264 --> 00:19:27,443
Come on!

454
00:19:31,844 --> 00:19:32,900
Hello?!

455
00:19:34,720 --> 00:19:36,812
Hey. Excuse me! Cat toy guy!

456
00:19:37,657 --> 00:19:40,144
Can you get me
out of this thing? Ooh.

457
00:19:40,385 --> 00:19:42,690
Whoa. Your blood pressure
is really high.

458
00:19:42,818 --> 00:19:44,108
Oh, you think?!

459
00:19:46,914 --> 00:19:48,799
Dad, are you okay?

460
00:19:48,922 --> 00:19:49,878
Nope.

461
00:19:49,965 --> 00:19:52,728
That kid <i>freaked out.</i>

462
00:19:56,481 --> 00:19:57,843
Brick.

463
00:19:57,951 --> 00:19:58,961
Are you awake?

464
00:19:59,040 --> 00:20:01,394
No. Go to sleep.

465
00:20:01,494 --> 00:20:02,895
Will you play with me
again tomorrow?

466
00:20:02,979 --> 00:20:03,968
No.

467
00:20:04,058 --> 00:20:05,136
Pretty please?

468
00:20:05,235 --> 00:20:06,249
Pretty no.

469
00:20:12,660 --> 00:20:14,171
I called the 800 number
on the chair...

470
00:20:14,280 --> 00:20:16,445
But they're closed
till morning.

471
00:20:16,533 --> 00:20:19,557
Well, just unplug it, then!

472
00:20:20,166 --> 00:20:21,327
Oh, you looking at me?!

473
00:20:21,644 --> 00:20:24,208
Yeah, go ahead.
Take a <i>good, long</i> look,

474
00:20:24,307 --> 00:20:27,592
'cause this is what happens
if you wake up to life too late!

475
00:20:27,690 --> 00:20:30,050
And then you try to get your business
done and you can't!

476
00:20:30,214 --> 00:20:32,912
And nobody wants a gymnastics
teacher without any experience.

477
00:20:33,012 --> 00:20:35,781
They don't! I tried!
I tried everything!

478
00:20:35,864 --> 00:20:37,277
Frankie?

479
00:20:37,810 --> 00:20:38,911
Reverend Hayver.

480
00:20:39,002 --> 00:20:40,183
What's wrong?

481
00:20:40,286 --> 00:20:41,734
I'm stuck.

482
00:20:41,821 --> 00:20:46,164
You know, this reminds me
of a time when I was 9 years old

483
00:20:46,260 --> 00:20:48,043
and I got my knee caught

484
00:20:48,149 --> 00:20:51,769
in the banister
at my great Aunt Margo's house.

485
00:20:52,404 --> 00:20:54,391
It was quite uncomfortable,

486
00:20:54,480 --> 00:20:59,449
but I realized it taught me the divine
virtue of patience.

487
00:20:59,628 --> 00:21:01,940
If I recall correctly,

488
00:21:02,024 --> 00:21:04,734
the first hour
was the most trying.

489
00:21:04,954 --> 00:21:07,785
As I languished there
on the staircase,

490
00:21:07,880 --> 00:21:10,744
counting by the endless...

491
00:21:10,823 --> 00:21:12,036
<i>That's when I realized</i>
<i>why I'd been going</i>

492
00:21:12,125 --> 00:21:13,677
<i>to Reverend Hayver</i>
<i>all these years.</i>

493
00:21:13,818 --> 00:21:16,468
<i>It was the most relaxing hour</i>
<i>of my week.</i>

494
00:21:16,553 --> 00:21:19,242
...in the days of Ampharel
king of Shinar,

495
00:21:19,350 --> 00:21:21,468
Arioch king of Ellasar...

496
